# The core's shared-bus surface is TIED OFF in every riscv-formal harness in
# this directory, and this file is the declaration of that boundary.
#
# It is a BASELINE in exactly the sense formal/INTERRUPT_TIE_OFF is:
# formal/check-multihart-tie-off.py exits 0 only when the sets below match what
# is on disk EXACTLY, in both directions, and it re-derives the port half from
# rtl/littlecpu.v rather than believing it. A harness added without a line
# fails; a line here with no harness fails too; and a port tied to a constant at
# every harness with no line here fails, because that is a restriction arriving
# unrecorded.
#
# WHAT IS TIED OFF. Three inputs, all of them the second hart's half of a
# conversation there is no second hart to have:
#
#   bus_wait     the shared bus is somebody else's this cycle
#   snoop_write  another bus initiator is writing
#   snoop_addr   ...and where
#
# `mem_lock` is the fourth port of that surface and is deliberately NOT here.
# It is an OUTPUT: a harness that leaves it unread cannot weaken a check, and
# what it says is proved by formal/components.sby's accessor task, where the
# lock is asserted to cover exactly the cycle an AMO writes back.
#
# WHY THE TIE-OFF. riscv-formal at formal/pin.mk's SHA describes ONE hart. Its
# channels are one core's retires, it models no second agent on memory, and no
# check upstream names a foreign write, a bus grant or a reservation. So a free
# `snoop_write` would clear reservations for reasons the spec model cannot see,
# and a free `bus_wait` would not weaken the checks so much as point them at a
# machine their spec does not describe -- an environment allowed to withhold the
# grant forever holds the core still forever, which is exactly what `hang` and
# `liveness_ch0` measure.
#
# THE DEPTHS ARE DERIVED UNDER IT, and this is the half that is easy to miss.
# Every `[depth]` line in formal/checks.cfg comes from F (worst-case first
# retire) and G (worst-case retire gap), both measured on the machine these
# harnesses build -- the SINGLE-HART configuration, where all three inputs are
# constants and the seventh stall reason can never rise. An ungranted cycle
# issues nothing, so in a dual configuration G grows by however long a hart may
# wait for the bus, and every depth built on G with it. That configuration has
# no generated checks at all today: nothing here builds two cores, so nothing
# here has a G to measure for one. When something does, its depths are a fresh
# derivation and not these numbers.
#
# WHEN THIS GOES RED. A harness that stops tying one of these off, a port
# renamed in rtl/littlecpu.v, or a new port tied to a constant everywhere and
# not written down. Do not edit the lines below to make that go away: the last
# case is a restriction on what the generated checks cover, and the whole point
# of a baseline is that such a restriction is declared rather than discovered.
#
# FORMAT -- whitespace-separated records:
#
#     HARNESS   <path>              a file in formal/ that instantiates
#                                   littlecpu. It must tie off every PORT below.
#     PORT      <name> <value>      an input of rtl/littlecpu.v, and the exact
#                                   constant every harness connects to it.
#     ELSEWHERE <name> <baseline>   an input tied to a constant at every harness
#                                   whose restriction is recorded in another
#                                   file. Without this the check below could not
#                                   tell a declared tie-off from an undeclared
#                                   one.

HARNESS  wrapper.v
HARNESS  complete.sv
HARNESS  cover.sv
HARNESS  dmemcheck.sv
HARNESS  imemcheck.sv

PORT bus_wait    1'b0
PORT snoop_write 1'b0
PORT snoop_addr  32'b0

# The interrupt, tied off for its own reasons in its own baseline. It is named
# here only so the sweep for undeclared tie-offs has one set to compare against.
ELSEWHERE irq_timer INTERRUPT_TIE_OFF
